<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Etihad Airways</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2003">2003</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Etihad Airways is the national airline of the United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, founded by royal decree in 2003 and based at Abu Dhabi International Airport. Owned by the government of Abu Dhabi, it grew rapidly into a major long-haul carrier connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, Asia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> through Abu Dhabi, and in the 2010s bought stakes in airlines such as Alitalia and Air <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> before cutting back.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/etihad-airways.jpg" alt="Etihad Airways" width="320" height="187" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Acroterion · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Etihad_Boeing_787-9_A6-BNI_IAD_VA2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> of Abu Dhabi had been a joint owner of Gulf Air with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, Qatar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, and Abu Dhabi's airport was one of Gulf Air's bases until the United Arab Emirates withdrew in 2005. In July 2003 Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalifa-bin-zayed-al-nahyan">Khalifa bin Zayed Al Nahyan</a>, who wanted an airline for Abu Dhabi, issued an Amiri decree establishing Etihad Airways as a national airline. Sheikh Ahmed bin Saif Al Nahyan founded it with start-up capital of 500 million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirham">dirhams</a>. Etihad means union or unity in Arabic and stands for the unity of the seven emirates. Services began with a ceremonial flight to Al Ain on 5 November 2003, and commercial operations started on 12 November with flights to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In June 2004 the airline ordered six Boeing 777-200s and 24 Airbus aircraft, including ten A380s, worth US$8 billion. James Hogan, formerly chief executive of Gulf Air, led it from 2006, and it reported its first full-year net profit, of US$14 million, in 2011.</p><p>From December 2011 Etihad built an equity alliance of minority stakes in other carriers: 29.21 percent of Air Berlin, 40 percent of Air Seychelles, 49 percent of the Serbian carrier that became Air Serbia, and in 2014 49 percent of Alitalia. Losses on Air Berlin and Alitalia mounted, and both partners later went bankrupt, which led Etihad to cut back its investments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Etihad is owned by Abu Dhabi and has its head office in Khalifa City, Abu Dhabi, near the airport that serves as its hub; the head office and training centre were completed in 2007. A board of seven independent non-executive members governs the airline under its founding legislation. Besides passenger services it runs Etihad Cargo and Etihad Holidays.</p><p>The airline was title sponsor of the 2009 Abu Dhabi Grand Prix, and it introduced a three-room cabin called The Residence on its A380s, which was then the only one of its kind on any airline. The Etihad Guest frequent flyer programme began on 30 August 2006.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Etihad Airways” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
